pancake treats performance as a public contract, but it enforces that contract two
different ways, and the difference is the whole point of this page. Where a cost can be
pinned to an exact number, a build gate holds the number. Where it cannot, because wall-clock
throughput moves with your CPU, your build mode, and your traffic, pancake reports the
figure instead of asserting it, and never lets an adjective stand in for a measurement.
So there are no "N times faster" claims anywhere else on this site; the place a speed
number is allowed to live is a benchmark you can run, and that is this page.

## What the build gates

These are not benchmarks. They are assertions that fail `zig build check` if they drift.

- **One allocation per delivery.** A steady-state inbound `op:deliver` to a live local
  object, entered through the borrowed-frame path, costs exactly one heap allocation: the
  owned frame copy the event model keeps. The alloc-budget test gates that with a `== 1`
  assertion, so a stray allocation on that hot path breaks the build. This gate is the
  inbound-delivery floor; the opening handshake and promise pipelining carry their own
  separately measured costs.
- **The rest of the wire-path budget.** The same test suite gates more than that floor.
  Outbound `encodeMessage` is held to its allocation count, an `op:resolve` record to a
  single allocation with no resolution copy, the model value-tree struct sizes to fixed
  ceilings, GC export churn to a flat live heap, and a steady hosted-vat turn to zero
  vat-allocator allocations. Each one fails `zig build check` on regression.
- **Per-connection footprint.** A footprint test holds the protocol-core state of a
  warmed connection, one that has processed a delivery, under a fixed ceiling of about two
  kilobytes. The goal it serves is a node carrying on the order of half a million sessions,
  where memory is the binding constraint, so the ceiling is a design budget the core is
  built to fit, not a measurement of a node under real socket load. The
  [ladder](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/ladder.md) owns the
  exact number and the arithmetic behind it.

## What the benchmarks report

These print numbers; they do not gate them. No test asserts a wall-clock bound, because a
defensible one needs a stable, release-pinned harness that does not exist yet. Every chart
and table below is regenerated from live runs by `zig build bench-site-report` — a dated
snapshot, not a standing claim.

```=html
<!-- bench:stamp GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<p class="bench-stamp"><em>Measured 2026-06-20 on one machine: Linux 6.12.76-linuxkit aarch64, 16 logical CPUs, 8 GiB RAM, Racket v9.2 CS, Spritely Goblins v0.18.0, with the <code>pancake</code> archives built by Zig 0.16.0 in ReleaseFast. One person's numbers, measure your own with the commands below.</em></p>
```

Two cautions, both straight from the harnesses:

- **Build mode dominates.** Under `zig build check` the benchmarks run in Debug, where
  throughput is roughly an order of magnitude below `ReleaseFast`. The numbers here are
  ReleaseFast; the `mode=` field in raw output tells you which you are reading. Debug
  numbers are for catching a regression against the previous Debug run.
- **Footprint is core state, not socket RSS.** The peer-scaling number is the heap of the
  sans-io core — the protocol engine that carries no sockets of its own — which is the
  dominant per-connection cost once read buffers come from a shared pool rather than
  per-connection. Real socket-level RSS and syscall rate under a live 500k load still need a
  measurement pancake has not run, and the page says so plainly.

## Codec throughput

[Syrup]($link.page('codecs')) packs a message into compact, variable-length byte records;
Cap'n Proto lays the same message out as larger, fixed-width words with every field at a
known offset. `bench-codec` times encode and decode of one representative call — a method
invocation with a small mixed-type argument list — on each codec, so the per-message
nanoseconds are comparable even though the MB/s figures move with frame size. This is a
small message on purpose: Cap'n Proto's edge is constant-time access over large or
field-heavy payloads, a shape this does not measure, and the trade is the wire size you see
here. The [codecs]($link.page('codecs')) page weighs the full picture. Measure your own:
`zig build bench-codec -Doptimize=ReleaseFast`.

```=html
<!-- bench:codec GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
