## Time-travel: snapshot and restore

pancake can take a snapshot of a vat's local actors and rewind to it later, a save point you can
return to. It is the same local time-travel [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/)
offers, with one difference: in pancake the feature is off by default, and you turn it on only for
the actors you want to be able to rewind. An actor you never snapshot pays nothing for it.

Taking a snapshot is cheap because it shares each actor's state instead of copying it. The cost
grows with the number of actors you have turned the feature on for, not with how much each one
holds: snapshotting a thousand actors costs about the same whether each holds 64 bytes or 16 KB.
The charts below put pancake's snapshot and restore beside Goblins' across a range of actor counts.
Both share state the same way, so the bars are there to show that shared shape, not to crown a
winner. pancake runs in Zig and Goblins in Racket, and while its native code shows lower numbers at
most points, the two land close on restore at the largest counts, where the ordering can even flip.
A constant runtime gap is not a better design.

Measure your own with `zig build bench-snapshot` and `racket bench/goblins/snapshot.rkt`. Those
benchmarks also measure a deeper question for anyone who wants the implementation detail: what it
costs to change a snapshotted actor as the data it holds grows larger.

